A base unit in the International System of Units (SI) is one of the seven units upon which all other SI units are built. These seven base units include:
the metre (m) for length;
 the kilogram (kg)for mass; 
 the second (s) for time; 
 the ampere (A) for electric current; 
 the kelvin (K) for thermodynamic temperature; 
 and the mole (mol) for the amount of substance.
Base units may be combined using the SI multiples and submultiples to create derived units, such as voltage (V), pressure (Pa), or luminosity (cd).

